# Pathways Roadmap

Pathways is an exploratory music discovery application built around interactive
graphs. Rather than recommending music through a traditional algorithmic feed,
Pathways lets the user actively follow relationships between artists, bands,
collaborators, genres, labels, tracks, and other musical entities.

The roadmap below describes the current direction of the project. It is not a
fixed implementation plan and will evolve as new data sources and exploration
patterns are introduced.

---

## Current State

The first working exploration flow is implemented.

- [x] Import playlist data from CSV
- [x] Visualize playlist tracks as an interactive graph
- [x] Position tracks using audio features such as valence and energy
- [x] Display track metadata
- [x] Connect the Angular frontend to the ASP.NET Core backend
- [x] Integrate MusicBrainz artist search
- [x] Retrieve artist-to-artist relationships from MusicBrainz
- [x] Expand playlist nodes with related artists
- [x] Continue exploration by clicking newly discovered artist nodes
- [x] Prevent duplicate artist nodes

At this stage, it is possible to start from a playlist and follow MusicBrainz
relationships across multiple degrees of separation.

---

## Phase 1 – Graph Exploration & Navigation

The immediate priority is making larger explorations easier to understand and
control.

### Expand and collapse nodes

- [ ] Track whether a node is expanded or collapsed
- [ ] Collapse relations by clicking an expanded node again
- [ ] Handle shared nodes without removing connections used elsewhere
- [ ] Avoid unnecessary repeated API requests

### Exploration path

Keep track of how the user arrived at the current node.

Example:

`Pulp → Artist A → Artist B → Stereolab`

- [ ] Track the active exploration path
- [ ] Display the path as a breadcrumb/tracker
- [ ] Allow navigation back through the path
- [ ] Highlight the active path in the graph
- [ ] Consider saving interesting discovery paths

### Control graph growth

- [ ] Limit the number of relations expanded at once
- [ ] Add "show more" functionality
- [ ] Filter relations by type
- [ ] Improve handling of large graphs
- [ ] Explore focus modes for reducing visual noise

---

## Phase 2 – Richer Graph Semantics

The graph should communicate what its nodes and edges represent without
requiring the user to inspect every item individually.

### Node types

Extend the current node type model beyond tracks and generic artists.

Possible node types:

- Track
- Person
- Band / group
- Label
- Genre

### Visual differentiation

- [ ] Use different visual styles for different node types
- [ ] Add a graph legend
- [ ] Explore node size as an indicator of connectivity
- [ ] Consider displaying the number of known relations

For example, people, bands, labels, and genres could each have their own
recognizable visual identity.

### Relationship information

- [ ] Store relationship metadata on graph edges
- [ ] Display relationship types such as:
  - member of band
  - involved with
  - producer
  - performer
  - arranger
  - conductor
- [ ] Make the relationship that led to the current node visible
- [ ] Use relationship information in the exploration path

---

## Phase 3 – Artist & Entity Information

Different node types should provide information relevant to that entity rather
than sharing a generic metadata popup.

### Artist / band information

Possible information includes:

- Short artist description
- Associated bands / projects
- Collaborators
- Known or notable connections
- Associated genres
- Number and types of known relationships
- The relationship through which the artist was discovered

### Track information

Continue improving the existing track information, including:

- Track title
- Artist
- Release information
- Year
- Genre
- Audio characteristics where available

Additional data sources such as Wikidata or Wikipedia may later complement
MusicBrainz where richer descriptive information is useful.

---

## Phase 4 – Multiple Exploration Starting Points

Playlist upload should become one of several ways to begin exploring music.

### Artist and band search

- [ ] Search directly for an artist or band
- [ ] Create the selected result as a starting node
- [ ] Explore its relationships without requiring a playlist upload

### Labels

Explore music through record labels.

Example:

`Warp Records → artist → collaborator → another project`

- [ ] Add labels as graph entities
- [ ] Retrieve artists/releases associated with labels
- [ ] Navigate between labels and artists

### Genres

- [ ] Add genres as possible graph entities
- [ ] Explore artists associated with a genre
- [ ] Navigate between genres and artists
- [ ] Investigate how genre data from different sources should be combined

The long-term goal is to allow users to start almost anywhere in the musical
network and follow whichever path becomes interesting.

---

## Phase 5 – Discovery Filters

As more data sources and entity types are introduced, users should be able to
control the type of exploration they want.

Possible filters include:

- [ ] Time period / release year
- [ ] Relationship type
- [ ] Genre
- [ ] Label
- [ ] Number of degrees of separation
- [ ] Maximum relations per node
- [ ] Person / band / track / label visibility

Filters should help control the data flow without removing the possibility of
unexpected discoveries.

---

## Phase 6 – Save Discoveries

Exploration should eventually lead to something the user can keep.

- [ ] Select interesting tracks/artists discovered in the graph
- [ ] Maintain a temporary discovery collection
- [ ] Export discovered tracks as CSV
- [ ] Save interesting exploration paths
- [ ] Investigate direct Spotify playlist creation

Direct Spotify integration would require authentication and appropriate API
permissions and is therefore considered a later feature.

---

## Phase 7 – Playlist Tools & Analysis

Pathways may later expand beyond graph exploration into tools for understanding
a user's own music collection.

### Multiple playlists

- [ ] Import multiple playlists
- [ ] Merge playlists into a combined graph
- [ ] Compare playlists
- [ ] Identify shared and unique artists
- [ ] Explore relationships between otherwise separate playlists

### Playlist analysis

Possible analyses include:

- Genre distribution
- Release periods
- Artist frequency
- Audio-feature distributions
- Relationship clusters
- Highly connected artists
- Similarities and differences between playlists

The purpose of analytics should remain exploratory rather than simply producing
statistics.

---

## Longer-Term Direction

Pathways should evolve from a playlist visualization into a general music
exploration system.

A user might begin with:

`My playlist`

`Pulp`

`Warp Records`

`Post-rock`

and move through a network of:

`tracks ↔ people ↔ bands ↔ collaborations ↔ labels ↔ genres`

The central idea remains the same:

**Start somewhere familiar. Follow the connections. Discover somewhere
unexpected.**
